==========================================================================
A2.2  mutation test: does the replay assert constrain the addressing?
==========================================================================
  features.py docstring: '`replay` asserts its own recurrence
  reproduces the shipped forward pass.' The question is whether that
  assert gives any coverage of the ADDRESSING claim stated two
  paragraphs above it in the same docstring.

  corruption applied:  addr = tokens[:, t - 1]  ->  tokens[:, t]

  result: MUTANT_ASSERT_PASSED

  DEFECT CONFIRMED: the assert does NOT detect a corrupted address.
  The bookkeeping arrays (last_e, sum_e, cnt, ndist, valset,
  last_val) are pure side outputs: nothing in them feeds S or
  the logits, so the assert cannot see them. It verifies the
  recurrence and gives ZERO coverage of the addressing.

==========================================================================
A2 VERDICT
==========================================================================
  A2.1 bookkeeping vs task `seen`      PASS
  A2.3 independent reimplementation    PASS
  A2.4 class labelling                 PASS
  A2.2 assert coverage of addressing   DEFECT (no coverage)

  So: the addressing as SHIPPED is correct, and the assert cited
  for it proves nothing about it.
